#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Radioactive Decay Given the Half-Life of a Substance"}] :question/id #uuid "0681419e-fc16-53ad-89b9-945fb616a391" :question/math-academy-id "e-6675" :question/problem "The half-life of bismuth-210 is $5.012$ days. How long does it take for a $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ sample of bismuth-210 to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 5.012\n$$\n\ndays we have\n\n$$\n5.012 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5.012}\n$$\n\nAlso, since\n\n$$\nM_0 = 1\\,280\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nwe have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t) = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n5 = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= 1280e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{1280} &= e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{256} &= e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012} \\\\\n\\ln (\\frac{1}{256}) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}) \\\\\n\\ln 1 - \\ln 256 &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{5.012} \\\\\n- \\ln 256 &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{5.012} \\\\\nt &= \\frac{5.012 \\cdot \\ln 256}{\\ln 2} \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(M(t))/(M(0)) &= \\frac{5g}{1280g} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{256} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^8\\cdot M(0)\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $8$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ to $5\\,\\textrm g$. Hence,\n\n$$\nt = 8\\cdot t_{1/2} =8 \\cdot 5.012 \\approx 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716" :knowledge-point/title "Using Radioactive Dating to Estimate the Age of an Object"}] :question/id #uuid "67fa57e9-812f-54b7-b1d3-4d9e3fb99a1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9373" :question/problem "At a geological site, an object is found with $65\\%$ of its original chlorine-36 content. Given that the half-life of chlorine-36 is $301$ thousand years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 301\n$$\n\nthousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 0.65 M_0\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65 &= e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/301}) \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{301} \\\\\nt &= - (301 \\cdot \\ln (0.65))/(\\ln 2) \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 187\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "589c680e-9f93-58e9-9dab-c34de98d7225" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Half-Life Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "adf49e6f-28cc-55b4-9a46-3acc34fa6c6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9374" :question/problem "The mass $M(t)$, in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$M(t) = 100 e^{-kt}$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 2.6\n$$\n\nmillion years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267\n$$"}]}
